\description{
Computes the large-sample variance of the sample Pearson correlation
under bivariate normality and the variance of its Fisher \emph{Z}
transform. This standalone function is useful for AIPE planning,
meta-analytic weighting, and Wald-style inference on \emph{r}.
}
\details{
The variance of \emph{r} is evaluated at the population correlation
\code{true_cor} and the sample size \code{N}. Substituting a sample
correlation for \code{true_cor} gives a plug-in estimate of that
variance, which carries the sampling error of the correlation it was
computed from. The variance of the Fisher \emph{Z} transform,
\eqn{1/(N - 3)}, depends on \code{N} alone.


\strong{Normal-theory variance (default).} Under bivariate normality
the large-sample variance is
\deqn{\mathrm{Var}(\hat r) \;\approx\; (1 - \rho^2)^2 / (N - 1),}
the leading term of the exact moment expansion (Hotelling, 1953,
Section 7; the exact density of \eqn{r} is Fisher's, 1915).
As \eqn{N} grows, \eqn{N} times this variance approaches
\eqn{(1 - \rho^2)^2}, the variance of the limiting distribution of
\eqn{\sqrt{N}(\hat r - \rho)}. Carried to the Fisher \eqn{Z} scale by
the delta method it becomes \eqn{1/(N - 1)}; the interval of
\code{\link{ci_r}} uses Fisher's \eqn{1/(N - 3)} on that scale instead
(see below).

\strong{Non-normal margins.} The normal-theory variance does not hold
when the margins are not normal: marginal kurtosis can change the
large-sample variance of \eqn{r} considerably when \eqn{\rho \ne 0}
(Bonett & Wright, 2000, p. 24), and in general the variance depends on
every fourth-order cumulant of the joint distribution (Duncan & Layard,
1973, Equation 1.1).

\strong{Connection to Fisher's Z transform.} On the variance-
stabilized scale \eqn{Z = \tanh^{-1}(r)} the asymptotic variance is
\eqn{1/(N-3)} regardless of \eqn{\rho} (Fisher, 1921). This is
reported alongside the raw-scale variance because it is the natural
working scale for CI construction (\code{\link{ci_r}}).
}
\examples{
# 1. Normal-theory variance:
